uid "2daf57e1-a02d-5e5f-b4a4-bb8ae9638a75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179858 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179858 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179858 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179858 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179858 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179858 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179858 #edb/ref 1045 "Sums of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Over Parametric Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179858 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179858 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179858 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179858 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e609e5db-3168-57af-8ec3-6779df22874b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180285 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186179254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180285 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180285 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180285 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{y}{x}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180285 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7db959c5-fcb6-5723-b0e1-39e452e905f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180285 #edb/ref 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{y}{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}-0]-[0-4] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180394 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180394 #edb/ref 1061 26175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180394 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1221b846-bf07-5b93-bf5a-5f8ba64ac365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180394 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180395 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180395 #edb/ref 1061 27289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180395 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "621fd314-933c-56a6-97f4-7a591c8ceee3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180395 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180476 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180476 #edb/ref 1061 35620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180476 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3d3c67b4-ef9a-5648-81cf-8ed48d14fa68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180476 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180477 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180477 #edb/ref 1061 35621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180477 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "643f2b27-0aa4-53bd-995f-f4862424f404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180477 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180487 #edb/ref 1061 35687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180487 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3d36521-e553-55b3-9ce0-83868054277e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180487 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180681 #edb/ref 1064 "Line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y$ often come in pairs. For example, \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int\\limits_C (x-y) \\, \\textrm dy.\n$$\n\nWe usually express a sum of line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y$ as a single integral. In the case above, we can write this sum as\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm dx + (x-y) \\, \\textrm dy.\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate this integral in this case where $C$ is given by the line segment\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\underbrace{(6t+1)}_{x(t)} \\,\\mathbf{i} + \\underbrace{(2t+1)}_{y(t)} \\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t\\in\\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nAlong the path $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 6t+1, \\qquad y = 2t+1.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the line integral by applying a change of variables, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t+1+2t+1)\\cdot 6+(6t+1-(2t+1))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\cdot 6+(4t)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}48t+12+8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}56t+12\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={28{t}^{2}+12t|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =28+12 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180681 #edb/ref 1065 4437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180681 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7c3e84f3-cbc8-5522-87f2-5accaa3854e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180681 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180712 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function of three variables and $C$ be a path in three-dimensional space. Suppose we want to calculate the following sum of line integrals:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f \\, \\textrm dx + \\int\\limits_C f \\, \\textrm dy +  \\int\\limits_C f \\, \\textrm dz\n$$\n\nFor notational simplicity, we can express this as a single integral, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f \\, \\textrm dx + f \\, \\textrm dy +   f \\, \\textrm dz\n$$\n\nAs before, we can evaluate this integral by parameterizing the curve $C$ using the parameter $t\\in [a,b]$ and then rewriting the integral using a change of variables, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f\\,\\text{d}x+f\\,\\text{d}y+f\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+f\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+f\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(f\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+f\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+f\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we often omit the large parentheses and simply write\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + f \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} +   f \\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} \\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nFinally, it can be helpful to switch to prime notation for the derivatives when dealing with complex integrands. In this case, the above formula becomes\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f x'+ f y' +   f z' \\,\\textrm d t,\n$$\n\nwhere the primes denote differentiation with respect to $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180712 #edb/ref 1